---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { projects } from '../data/projects';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<Layout>
  <section class="hero container">
    <div class="hero-copy"><p class="eyebrow"><span class="status-dot"></span> ROBOTICS & ENGINEERING · PHD RESEARCHER</p>
      <h1>Robot swarms.<br /><em>RF awareness.</em></h1>
      <p class="hero-intro">I'm <strong>Adam Morris</strong>, an Engineering Mathematics PhD researcher developing robot swarms for RF monitoring and concealed-emitter localisation.</p>
      <p class="hero-detail">Exploring autonomous sensing for defence and security through simulation and physical robotic systems.</p>
      <div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href="#projects">Explore my work <span>↗</span></a><a class="text-link" href="#contact">Get in touch <span>↗</span></a></div>
      <div class="hero-meta"><span>University of Bristol</span><span>Bristol, UK</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-visual"><div class="visual-top"><span>ADAM MORRIS</span></div><div class="portrait-placeholder"><img src={`${base}adam-morris-profile.png`} alt="Portrait of Adam Morris" width="1254" height="1254" fetchpriority="high" style="width:100%;height:100%;object-fit:cover;display:block" /></div><div class="visual-bottom"><span>Engineering Mathematics · University of Bristol</span></div></div>
  </section>
  <div class="expertise-strip"><div class="container"><span>RESEARCH FOCUS</span><span>Swarm robotics</span><span class="strip-plus">+</span><span>RF sensing</span><span class="strip-plus">+</span><span>Defence & security</span></div></div>
  <section id="about" class="container section about"><div><p class="eyebrow">01 / ABOUT</p><h2>Spatial intelligence<br />for secure environments.</h2></div><div class="about-body"><p class="large-copy">How can a team of robots build a better understanding of the electromagnetic environment?</p><p>My PhD investigates how teams of autonomous robots can monitor and maintain the RF domain in secure environments, including localising anomalous signals from concealed adversarial transmitters.</p><p>I combine 2D simulations for swarm design, high-fidelity 3D electromagnetic propagation modelling, and ROS 2/Gazebo for robot-control simulation. I also work with physical robotic platforms, including the LEO Rover and Clearpath Jackal.</p><div class="skill-groups"><div><h3>Proficient</h3><p>Python · MATLAB · LaTeX</p></div><div><h3>Experienced</h3><p>C/C++ · Arduino</p></div><div><h3>Simulation & platforms</h3><p>Gazebo · 2D swarm simulation · 3D EM modelling · LEO Rover · Clearpath Jackal</p></div><div><h3>Development tools</h3><p>Git · Linux · ROS 2</p></div><div><h3>Languages</h3><p>English & Welsh (fluent)</p></div><div><h3>Professional membership</h3><p>Member of IEEE</p></div></div></div></section>
  <section id="projects" class="projects-section"><div class="container section"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">02 / SELECTED PROJECTS</p><h2>Research & engineering projects.</h2></div><p><span class="muted">Selected work, with further case studies to follow. Project 01 is an illustrative placeholder.</span></p></div><div class="project-grid">{projects.map(p => <a class="project-card" href={`${base}projects/${p.slug}/`}><div class={`project-image ${p.type} ${'real' in p ? 'project-image-real' : ''}`}>{'swarm' in p && p.swarm ? <><span class="image-index">PROJECT / {p.number}</span><img class="project-card-evidence project-card-poster" src={`${base}${p.thumbnail}`} alt="Overhead arena photo of three Leo Rovers patrolling among box obstacles" width="800" height="450" loading="lazy" /></> : 'image' in p ? <><span class="image-index">PROJECT / {p.number}</span><img class="project-card-evidence" src={`${base}${p.image}`} alt="Wheel-speed and error plots from the PID tuning experiment" width="800" height="390" loading="lazy" /></> : <><span class="image-index">PROJECT / {p.number}</span><span class="image-placeholder">[ PROJECT IMAGE ]</span><span class="image-caption">{p.type.toUpperCase()} STUDY</span><span class="image-arrow">↗</span></>}</div><div class="project-content"><p class="eyebrow">{p.category}</p><h3>{p.title}<span>↗</span></h3><p>{p.summary}</p><div class="tags">{p.tags.map(tag => <span>{tag}</span>)}</div></div></a>)}</div>
    <section id="tools" class="tools-section" aria-labelledby="tools-title">
      <div class="tools-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">TOOLS</p><h3 id="tools-title">Custom Tools</h3></div><p class="muted">Side project apps and utilities for convenience. Built in conjunction with AI tools such as Codex and Claude Code.</p></div>
      <div class="tool-grid">
        <article class="tool-card">
          <img class="tool-screenshot-image" src={`${base}tools/patrol-graph-builder/patrol-graph-editor.png`} alt="Patrol Graph Builder editor showing an eight-waypoint patrol graph on a two-room occupancy map, with one edge flagged red for crossing a wall and one one-way edge marked by an arrow" loading="lazy" />
          <div class="tool-content"><p class="eyebrow">TOOL</p><h4>Patrol Graph Builder</h4><details class="tool-description"><summary><span class="show-description">Show description</span><span class="hide-description">Hide description</span></summary><p>I built a browser-based editor for drawing robot patrol routes onto ROS (Robot Operating System) occupancy-grid maps. It loads a PGM map with its map_server YAML, so waypoints sit in the map frame in metres. You click and drag to place waypoints, then connect them into a graph with two-way or one-way edges; edges passing through walls are flagged. Graphs export as adjacency JSON (positions plus neighbour lists) for a patrol planner, and a <code>patrol-build</code> terminal command opens the editor with a map preloaded.</p></details><div class="tags"><span>JavaScript</span><span>HTML Canvas</span><span>Python</span><span>ROS 2</span></div></div>
        </article>
        <article class="tool-card">
          <img class="tool-screenshot-image" src={`${base}tools/superiorpaper/superiorpaper-app.png`} alt="SuperiorPaper settings window showing a three-monitor layout preview with a different wallpaper on each screen and a profile list for monitor setups" loading="lazy" />
          <div class="tool-content"><p class="eyebrow">TOOL</p><h4 class="tool-title"><img src={`${base}tools/superiorpaper/superiorpaper-icon.png`} alt="" loading="lazy" />SuperiorPaper</h4><details class="tool-description"><summary><span class="show-description">Show description</span><span class="hide-description">Hide description</span></summary><p>I built SuperiorPaper, a Linux desktop app that gives each monitor its own wallpaper and keeps a profile for every monitor setup, such as laptop-only or docked at a desk. GNOME allows only one wallpaper, so it composes a single image matching the monitor layout. A background service recognises monitors by their hardware IDs and switches profiles automatically when screens are plugged in, notifying me about new setups. The settings window previews the layout to scale; clicking a screen picks its image from a built-in wallpaper library.</p></details><p class="tool-credit">Superiorpaper was inspired by <a href="https://github.com/hhannine/superpaper">Superpaper</a>.</p><div class="tags"><span>Python</span><span>GTK4</span><span>libadwaita</span><span>D-Bus</span><span>systemd</span></div></div>
        </article>
        <article class="tool-card">
          <img class="tool-screenshot-image tool-screenshot-tall" src={`${base}tools/ledger-local-finance-tracker/ledger-overview.png`} alt="Ledger dashboard showing spending cards, category trends, and vendor navigation; the displayed finance data is sample data" loading="lazy" />
          <div class="tool-content"><p class="eyebrow">TOOL</p><h4>Ledger</h4><details class="tool-description"><summary><span class="show-description">Show description</span><span class="hide-description">Hide description</span></summary><p>I built Ledger, a local-first finance tracker for reviewing Chase UK PDF and CSV statements in GBP. It parses transactions, checks statement totals and balances, stores data in SQLite, and supports custom categories, merchant rules, bill providers, vendor comparisons, spending trends and cash-flow views. Everything runs on the user’s computer; imported PDFs are processed locally and the app can be launched from the terminal with <code>financetracker</code>.</p></details><div class="tags"><span>Python</span><span>SQLite</span><span>PDF parsing</span><span>JavaScript</span><span>local-first</span></div></div>
        </article>
        <article class="tool-card">
          <img class="tool-screenshot-image" src={`${base}tools/tsr-hours-tracker/tsr-hours-calendar.png`} alt="TSR Hours calendar showing completed and planned teaching sessions, per-unit totals and approval-email export actions" loading="lazy" />
          <div class="tool-content"><p class="eyebrow">TOOL</p><h4>TSR Hours</h4><details class="tool-description"><summary><span class="show-description">Show description</span><span class="hide-description">Hide description</span></summary><p>I built TSR Hours to record teaching-support work against reusable unit names and codes. It provides a monthly calendar for teaching sessions, quarter-hour start and end time selection, automatic duration calculation, planned versus completed status, monthly miscellaneous work, unit totals, yearly charts, editable records and approval-email drafts. Entries are stored per signed-in user in Cloudflare D1; elapsed timed sessions complete automatically using UK time. I use it by adding units once, logging sessions or monthly work, then reviewing totals and exporting completed hours for approval.</p></details><div class="tags"><span>React</span><span>TypeScript</span><span>Cloudflare D1</span><span>Recharts</span></div></div>
        </article>
        <article class="tool-card">
          <img class="tool-screenshot-image tool-screenshot-tall" src={`${base}tools/statement-redactor/statement-redactor-interface.png`} alt="Statement Redactor interface showing local PDF selection, saved items, marking controls and preview workflow" loading="lazy" />
          <div class="tool-content"><p class="eyebrow">TOOL</p><h4>Statement Redactor</h4><details class="tool-description"><summary><span class="show-description">Show description</span><span class="hide-description">Hide description</span></summary><p>I built Statement Redactor to prepare bank statements for sharing without sending them to a service. It runs locally in the browser, lets users draw redaction areas or find repeated text, and creates a separate PDF copy after removing marked text, images and intersecting graphics. It also strips metadata, annotations, form fields, links and embedded attachments, then provides a preview and extracted-text check before download.</p></details><div class="tags"><span>Python</span><span>PyMuPDF</span><span>PDF security</span><span>local-first</span></div></div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div></section>
  <section id="background" class="container section"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">03 / BACKGROUND</p><h2>Education & experience.</h2></div><a class="text-link" href={`${base}cv/`}>View CV <span>↗</span></a></div><div class="background-grid"><div><h3 class="column-title">Education</h3><article class="timeline-item"><p class="date">2024 — 2028 (EXPECTED)</p><h4>PhD in Engineering Mathematics</h4><p class="institution">University of Bristol</p><p>Thesis: Swarm-Enabled Spatial Intelligence</p></article><article class="timeline-item"><p class="date">2020 — 2024</p><h4>MEng in Engineering Mathematics</h4><p class="institution">University of Bristol</p><p>First Class Honours · Dissertation: “Anti-aligning robots”</p></article></div><div><h3 class="column-title">Experience</h3><article class="timeline-item"><p class="date">JUNE 2023 — PRESENT</p><h4>Consultant Engineer</h4><p class="institution">SportsViz · Part-time, remote</p><p>Researching novel data interpretation and artificial intelligence methods for elite sports performance analysis, in a project with the Football Association of Wales (FAW).</p><ul class="experience-points"><li>Authored technical reports reviewing the literature and setting out potential future research directions.</li><li>Demonstrated AI and data analysis capabilities for international-level sports performance analysis, engaging with clients from varied professional backgrounds.</li></ul></article><article class="timeline-item"><p class="date">SEPTEMBER 2023 — PRESENT</p><h4>Graduate Teaching Support</h4><p class="institution">University of Bristol</p><p>Support undergraduate and master’s students across a range of units and assignments.</p><ul class="experience-points"><li>Provide academic support in technical writing and research methodologies, including marking and one-to-one supervision of a master’s dissertation.</li><li>Quickly develop an understanding of unfamiliar topics to support student work, and coordinate meetings across multiple student groups.</li></ul></article></div></div></section>
  <section class="container section publications"><p class="eyebrow">04 / PUBLICATIONS</p><h2>Published research.</h2><article class="publication"><span class="date">SSRR<br/>2025</span><div><h3>Spatially Intelligent Patrol Routes for Concealed Emitter Localization by Robot Swarms</h3><p><strong>Adam Morris</strong>, Timothy Pelham, Edmund R. Hunt</p><p>IEEE International Symposium on Safety, Security, and Rescue Robotics · Galway, Ireland · 29–31 October 2025</p><p><a href="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/11391260">IEEE Xplore ↗</a></p></div></article></section>
  <section id="contact" class="contact-section"><div class="container contact-inner"><div><p class="eyebrow">05 / CONTACT</p><h2>Let's talk robotics<br />& RF sensing.</h2><p>I welcome conversations about autonomous systems, defence and security research, engineering opportunities, and collaboration.</p></div><div class="contact-links"><a href="mailto:adamrhysmorris@gmail.com">adamrhysmorris@gmail.com <span>↗</span></a><div class="social-placeholders"><a href="https://github.com/ks20447">GitHub ↗</a><a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/adam-morris-eng-maths/">LinkedIn ↗</a><a href="https://scholar.google.com/citations?hl=en&amp;user=NnjMzeMAAAAJ">Google Scholar ↗</a></div><div class="website-qr"><a href="https://ks20447.github.io/" aria-label="Open Adam Morris’s website"><img src={`${base}adam-morris-website-qr.png`} width="160" height="160" alt="QR code for https://ks20447.github.io/" loading="lazy" /></a><div><h3>Open on your phone</h3><p>Scan with your phone’s camera.</p><a href={`${base}adam-morris-website-qr.png`} download="Adam-Morris-Website-QR.png">Download QR code ↓</a></div></div></div></div></section>
</Layout>
